(docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #135: kill(2) reads the signal number as a 32-bit
 int, so 4294967305 would reach it as 9, SIGKILL, and 4294967296 as 0. A
 signal number outside 0 to 64 that only the running program knows sets
 the error flag and sends nothing: the forked child is still running
 after both, and is then stopped with signal 9 and reaped.)

a number called oversized is 0.
Set oversized to 4294967305.
a number called wrapped is 0.
Set wrapped to 4294967296.

Set pid to fork the process.
If pid is 0 then,
    Wait 30 seconds.
If pid is greater than 0 then,
    Send signal oversized to process pid.
    On error print "signal 4294967305 refused".
    Send signal wrapped to child pid.
    On error print "signal 4294967296 refused".
    Set finished to reap process pid without waiting.
    If finished is 0 then,
        Print "the child is still running".
    Send signal 9 to process pid.
    Set reaped to reap process pid.
    If reaped is pid then,
        Print "the child is stopped and reaped".
